1
00:00:01,040 --> 00:00:06,560
Hi everyone. In today's video, we will perform unit testing using GTest.

2
00:00:06,560 --> 00:00:19,700
First, I have a list of C files and items that need to be tested using GTest.

3
00:00:19,700 --> 00:00:30,440
Here, I will select the first item to demonstrate how to perform UT.

4
00:00:30,440 --> 00:00:35,910
To be able to write GTest testcases,

5
00:00:35,910 --> 00:00:45,830
we first need to create a test environment for this item. Currently, there are two ways to do this.

6
00:00:45,830 --> 00:00:52,680
The first way is the manual method. We need to prepare a folder with the same name as the item to be tested,

7
00:00:52,680 --> 00:01:10,500
and this folder must contain the corresponding C file. Then we drag and drop that C file into the script.

8
00:01:10,500 --> 00:01:30,470
After that, the tool will automatically analyze it and generate a test environment with all required inputs, including the header files and the C++ file containing our test script.

9
00:01:30,470 --> 00:01:46,230
The second way is to use the tool.

10
00:01:46,230 --> 00:01:58,420
We use the tool by filling in the required fields, such as the database path, workspace path, and the items that need to be tested.

11
00:01:58,420 --> 00:02:18,700
After that, a complete message will appear, and a test environment similar to the manual method will be generated.

12
00:02:18,700 --> 00:02:35,700
It will also contain all the required files.

13
00:02:35,700 --> 00:02:45,360
After the environment has been created successfully using either of the two methods above,

14
00:02:45,360 --> 00:02:59,620
we will have all the input headers, the main header file, and the C++ file containing the stub functions, mock functions, and testcase design.

15
00:02:59,620 --> 00:03:08,390
However, a very common issue when working with UT using GTest

16
00:03:08,390 --> 00:03:16,090
is compiler errors caused by missing definitions of data types, structs, enums,

17
00:03:16,090 --> 00:03:25,160
or related headers, such as the console-related headers shown here.

18
00:03:25,160 --> 00:03:32,480
The solution is to use a second tool,

19
00:03:32,480 --> 00:03:54,820
which is the UT AI Cop tool. We need to provide the paths of the item,

20
00:03:54,820 --> 00:04:56,360
the item template, the database path, and the file tree. Then we click Auto Define Object.

21
00:04:56,360 --> 00:05:16,040
Instead of manually searching for each header file inside the database,

22
00:05:16,040 --> 00:05:26,920
we only need to provide some required information.

23
00:05:26,920 --> 00:05:38,660
Then the UT AI Cop tool will automatically search through the database, find the correct definitions,

24
00:05:38,660 --> 00:05:50,240
and insert the required information, data types, or files into the header file.

25
00:05:50,240 --> 00:05:58,240
After solving the data-related issues, we will build it again to check whether there are still any errors.

26
00:05:58,240 --> 00:06:00,730
We can do this by selecting the Build tab

27
00:06:00,730 --> 00:06:08,710
as shown on the screen.

28
00:06:08,710 --> 00:06:11,050
As you can see,

29
00:06:11,050 --> 00:06:13,350
the missing declaration errors

30
00:06:13,350 --> 00:06:14,610
are no longer shown.

31
00:06:14,610 --> 00:06:16,870
Instead, we now have

32
00:06:16,870 --> 00:06:17,970
undefined reference errors

33
00:06:17,970 --> 00:06:20,390
for some external functions.

34
00:06:20,390 --> 00:06:22,780
This is a sign

35
00:06:22,780 --> 00:06:25,000
that

36
00:06:25,000 --> 00:06:27,560
these external functions

37
00:06:27,560 --> 00:06:30,090
need to be stubbed

38
00:06:30,090 --> 00:06:32,270
or mocked to isolate

39
00:06:32,270 --> 00:06:32,790
the scope

40
00:06:32,790 --> 00:06:35,170
of the module we are testing.

41
00:06:35,170 --> 00:06:50,060
To handle the stub and mock generation for these functions, we can use the Repair and Get Stub function in the AI Cop tool.

42
00:07:03,780 --> 00:07:10,400
The tool has finished running. It has added two functions and mocked them.

43
00:07:14,440 --> 00:07:17,340
Now we will build it again to check.

44
00:07:21,110 --> 00:07:26,290
We will check whether the previous undefined reference errors still exist.

45
00:07:26,610 --> 00:07:35,770
Now there are no more undefined reference errors.

46
00:07:35,770 --> 00:07:38,190
Instead, the sample testcase

47
00:07:38,190 --> 00:07:39,390
shown below

48
00:07:39,390 --> 00:07:41,130
has been executed successfully.

49
00:07:41,130 --> 00:07:46,960
Next,

50
00:07:46,960 --> 00:07:48,960
we will design a testcase.

51
00:07:48,960 --> 00:07:52,940
After the issue with

52
00:07:52,940 --> 00:07:55,380
undefined reference functions

53
00:07:55,380 --> 00:07:56,580
has been resolved

54
00:07:56,580 --> 00:07:59,310
by stubbing

55
00:07:59,310 --> 00:08:01,190
and mocking those functions,

56
00:08:01,190 --> 00:08:03,130
the next step

57
00:08:03,130 --> 00:08:05,210
is to design

58
00:08:05,210 --> 00:08:05,910
a testcase.

59
00:08:05,910 --> 00:08:10,940
Here, I have a sample testcase.

60
00:08:10,940 --> 00:08:12,350
A standard testcase

61
00:08:12,350 --> 00:08:27,900
usually consists of six parts. The first part is the TEST macro.

62
00:08:27,900 --> 00:08:37,530
Inside it, there will be the test fixture and the testcase name.

63
00:08:37,530 --> 00:08:47,740
The purpose of the test fixture is to group related testcases together, while the testcase name is used to identify each testcase.

64
00:08:47,740 --> 00:09:00,650
The second part is the description. Here, we describe the testing purpose,

65
00:09:00,650 --> 00:09:16,480
such as which logic is being checked, what special input is used, or whether it covers the true branch of a certain condition.

66
00:09:16,480 --> 00:09:29,070
The third part is the input section. This is the preparation step,

67
00:09:29,070 --> 00:09:42,900
where we declare variables and assign the required input values before calling the tested function.

68
00:09:42,900 --> 00:09:53,300
The fourth part is the stub function section.

69
00:09:53,300 --> 00:10:04,850
If our function goes through sub-functions that were stubbed in the previous step,

70
00:10:04,850 --> 00:10:15,060
we need to set up the return values or call those functions according to the code flow.

71
00:10:15,060 --> 00:10:41,300
The fifth part is the call section, where we call the function that we need to test. Finally, we use EXPECT_EQ or other check functions to compare the actual result with the expected value.

72
00:10:41,300 --> 00:10:57,590
If they match, the result will be Pass. If the check result does not match,

73
00:10:57,590 --> 00:11:28,070
the result will be Fail. Now we will try building it, and there are a few errors here.

74
00:11:28,070 --> 00:11:38,440
The function I am calling, security level, is currently declared as a void type,

75
00:11:38,440 --> 00:12:13,530
but it has a return statement. So I will remove this return value.

76
00:12:13,530 --> 00:12:33,760
The first testcase has passed, and my variable checks are correct.

77
00:12:33,760 --> 00:12:44,540
However, we need to know how much of the function logic this testcase has covered.

78
00:12:44,540 --> 00:12:51,500
To answer this question, we will move to the next step,

79
00:12:51,500 --> 00:13:15,540
which is building the GTest coverage and using a third tool, the coverage IDE.

80
00:13:15,540 --> 00:13:47,330
We will add the coverage folder that we just built in order to view the coverage result.

81
00:13:47,330 --> 00:13:58,260
As you can see, this testcase is currently designed to cover the true case of this condition.

82
00:13:58,260 --> 00:14:40,030
This condition has been covered correctly. I will add one more testcase.

83
00:14:40,030 --> 00:15:42,360
This testcase will cover the false case of this condition at line 59. Then we will check it again.

84
00:15:42,360 --> 00:16:06,140
The second testcase has also passed, and the false condition has been covered.

85
00:16:06,140 --> 00:16:14,070
Similarly, for the remaining conditions,

86
00:16:14,070 --> 00:17:03,300
we need to add more testcases to cover each condition and achieve 100% coverage.

87
00:17:03,300 --> 00:17:18,880
I have just added the prepared testcases, and all testcases have passed. All 14 testcases passed successfully and achieved 100% coverage.

88
00:17:18,880 --> 00:17:30,240
Now we will check the GTest coverage again. All conditions have been fully covered.

89
00:17:30,240 --> 00:17:48,290
After the testcases have achieved 100% coverage,

90
00:17:48,290 --> 00:17:56,520
we will submit them for review and continue with the next UT steps, such as review and BDC.

91
00:17:56,520 --> 00:17:57,800
BDC.

92
00:17:57,800 --> 00:18:02,760
I will end this video here.

93
00:18:02,760 --> 00:18:04,440
This video

94
00:18:04,440 --> 00:18:06,860
has shown

95
00:18:06,860 --> 00:18:10,280
how to perform UT using GTest.

96
00:18:10,280 --> 00:18:11,200
Thank you.
